Teach ir3_compiler to write the variant out: -o FILE, a hakios::metal::Kernel fixture.

ir3_compiler prints a listing of what it compiled and keeps the binary to itself.
hakios-metal dispatches kernels with raw PM4, as Mesa's computerator does, and
needs the words together with the layout ir3 chose around them: where the driver
params go, which UBO ranges were pushed to consts and to where, the immediates
and their offset, the register ids of the ids the hardware loads, constlen,
instrlen, the register footprint. No listing carries all of that, and the AIR
path has no other place where the variant exists -- the frontend is reached only
through this tool. So -o FILE writes the variant as the same C++ fixture that
hakios-metal's tools/ir3-kernel writes for a text kernel (its
include/hakios-metal/kernel.hpp documents every field and the register that
reads it), and -s FILE carries the kernel text (the .ll) into it for the record.

The variant is assembled once: ir3_shader_assemble frees the constant data it
appends to the binary, so dump_info now takes the binary rather than calling it.

Applies after air-frontend.patch, air-ir3.patch and ir3-round-default.patch
(tools/air/mk-host.sh's order) on mesa-25.0.7, with the AIR frontend copied in.

-o also writes the Kernel's texture fields (textures[], numTextureSlots,
samplerMask) from a walk of the NIR before ir3 lowers it: which Metal texture
index the code samples (sam/saml/isaml through the texture table) or stores
to as an image (stib/ldib at IBO slot num_ssbos + index), with what dimension
and element class and width, and which sampler indices it names. The dispatch
sizes its tables from these, not from ir3's num_samp, which counts textures and
images together and ignores sampler indices (ir3_compiler_nir.c), nor from the
front end's resource list, which says what was declared and not what the code
does. The same walk lives in hakios-metal's src/compiler.cpp, and compiler_test
holds the two to each other over tools/kernels/*.ll. A sampled slot the front
end reserved for Metal's null texture (air_resource.null_texture) is marked as
such: the draw binds its own texture there, whatever the application bound.

-o is stage-aware (29 October): a VERTEX or FRAGMENT variant is written as a
hakios::metal::Stage (include/hakios-metal/render.hpp) -- the Kernel as its
first member, then inputs[] (slot, regid, compmask, inloc, sysval, bary, flat,
half) and outputs[] (slot, regid, half, and the output's declared class in the
pad: float or integer, read off the NIR's shader_out variables before lowering)
straight from ir3_shader_variant, and the regids and flags turnip's
tu6_emit_vpc / tu6_emit_fs_inputs / tu6_emit_fs_outputs read (vertex id,
position, the ij barycentrics, frag coord, face, the colour outputs with
ir3_get_output_regid's HALF_REG_ID split, depth/stencil/sample mask, total_in,
has_kill, per_samp...). The vertex-only and fragment-only fields are read from
their own stage only, because the slot namespaces collide (VARYING_SLOT_POS and
FRAG_RESULT_DEPTH are both 0). A compute variant is written as the Kernel it
was, byte for byte; any other stage is refused by name, where before 29 October
a VS came out as a compute-shaped Kernel and the tool exited 0.

For a vertex or fragment AIR input the NIR is lowered in turnip's order:
ir3_nir_lower_io_to_temporaries on the IO VARIABLES, nir_lower_io inside
ir3_nir_post_finalize (ir3_nir.c:755; tu_shader.cc:2527). The AIR path used to
lower IO first, so io_to_temporaries found nothing to copy and left orphan
temporaries whose undef stores nir_opt_undef happened to remove; a second
store_output to one slot would have won in ir3's setup_output. Compute keeps
the old order (it has no IO variables) and its fixtures do not change.
hakios-metal's compileStage runs the same passes in the same order, and
compiler_test holds each Stage compiled there to the fixture written here.

